Differences in Myoelectric and Body-Powered Upper-Limb Prostheses: Systematic Literature Review [1],Not mentioned in source.,2017 [1],Systematic review [1],Not mentioned in source.,Myoelectric and body-powered upper-limb prostheses [1],"Myoelectric prostheses: improved cosmesis and reduced phantom-limb pain; more accepted for light-intensity work [1]. Prosthetic selection should be individualized based on patient preferences, experience, and functional needs [1].","Conflicting evidence on functional performance between body-powered and myoelectric prostheses [1]. Body-powered advantages: durability, shorter training time, fewer adjustments, easier maintenance, better feedback; but control could be improved [1]. Insufficient evidence to declare a general advantage for either system [1]. Overall lack of empirical evidence on functional differences [1].",Not mentioned in source.
Differences in Myoelectric and Body-Powered Upper-Limb Prostheses: Systematic Literature Review Update 2013–2016 [2],Not mentioned in source.,2017 [2],Systematic review [2],Not mentioned in source.,"Myoelectric and body-powered prostheses are the primary focus; hybrid prostheses (e.g., DEKA arm) and sensory/haptic feedback are noted as emerging topics [2]","Discussion centers on user decision factors influencing prosthesis selection (cost, insurance, function, sensory feedback, cosmesis) and the need for evidence on functional differences; specific clinical indications are not detailed in the source [2]","Included three publications that support: (1) potential need for multiple control strategies to facilitate intuitive prosthetic control, and (2) value of task training in improving function; overall insufficient evidence that myoelectric or body-powered prostheses are superior [2]","Emerging technologies noted include hybrid prostheses (e.g., DEKA arm) and developments in sensation and haptic feedback; detailed advancements not provided in source [2]"
"Improvement on upper limb body-powered prostheses (1921–2016): A systematic review ",Not mentioned in source.,2018,Systematic review,"The paper analyzes work related to improvements of upper limb body-powered prostheses from 1921 to 2016, covering almost a century of developments and frequency of publications (approximately one every 3 years) .",Body-powered prostheses (focus); comparison mentions myoelectric technology as more extensively studied .,Not mentioned in source.,"Findings: Identified 155 papers (1921–2016), with 40 in the design and development category; ~81% involved mechanical alterations; 48% were patents; Journal of Rehabilitation Research and Development published most related articles; studies on improving body-powered arms are relatively few compared with myoelectric technology; recommendation for further research in underexplored areas .",Not mentioned in source.
Upper Limb Prostheses by the Level of Amputation: A Systematic Review [3],MDPI (Journal not explicitly named in full text PDF URL indicates MDPI journal) [3],2024 [3],Systematic review [3],"Discusses evolution over time focusing on year-of-publication trends, technology readiness levels, and prosthesis weight and DoF trends (e.g., analysis by year, TRL, weight) [3]","Covers passive, active (body-powered/mechanically actuated and electrically actuated), myoelectric (surface EMG predominant), hybrid (e.g., EMG-EEG), and invasive neural interfaces/tendon-driven concepts [3]","Focused on restoring activities of daily living across amputation levels, improving grasping and gestures, sensory/haptic feedback for naturalness, and addressing balance/weight for unilateral amputees [3]",Main conclusions: surface EMG is predominant for control; gap in TRL-8 devices indicating commercialization bottleneck; trend toward more gestures/DoF and better control but limited progress in reducing weight; accessibility and cost remain major barriers [3],"Highlights: advances in control (surface EMG, pattern recognition, ML), invasive neural interfaces (TMR, nerve or muscle implants), haptic/sensory feedback systems, 3D-printed polymer and carbon-fiber materials, and increasing DoF/gesture repertoires [3]"
A Review on Upper-Limb Myoelectric Prosthetic Control [4],Not mentioned in source.,2018 [4],Narrative review (state-of-the-art review) [4],Not mentioned in source.,Myoelectric prostheses (including multifunctional myoelectric prostheses with simultaneous and proportional control of multiple DoFs) [4],Discussion focuses on translation to clinical use and factors hindering clinical usability; current clinical adoption is limited [4],"Pattern-recognition based sEMG control enables intuitive multifunctional prosthesis control [4]; however, academic advances have not yet been adopted by end users [4]. Major barrier: achieving robust simultaneous and proportional control of multiple DoFs [4].","Advances in sEMG pattern classification for control, and control strategies for simultaneous and proportional control of multiple DoFs aimed at translating research into commercially robust myoelectric prostheses [4]"
Perspectives on the comparative benefits of body-powered and myoelectric upper limb prostheses [5],Journal of NeuroEngineering and Rehabilitation [5],2024 [5],Narrative review / Commentary [5],"Discusses recent technological evolution emphasizing emerging biosignal-based control approaches (muscles, peripheral nerves, brain) and states that despite advances many are not yet clinically available, leaving body-powered and myoelectric prostheses as the primary routinely prescribed options [5]","Covers body-powered (BP) and myoelectric (MYO) prostheses, including terminal device variations: voluntary-open/close hooks, single degree-of-freedom hands, multi-articulated hands; mentions MYO pattern recognition as an emerging control approach [5]","Clinical applications discussed include restoring function for activities of daily living (reaching, grasping, object manipulation), rehabilitation goals, prosthesis prescription decision-making, and considerations for insurance coverage and access to devices [5]","Finds insufficient evidence for consistent functional advantages of BP vs MYO prostheses; observed differences in sensory feedback did not translate to consistent differences in embodiment, movement quality, or kinematic patterns, and performance appears task- and user-dependent rather than device-dependent [5]","Highlights ongoing advances: biosignal-based control (muscle, nerve, brain interfaces), sensory restoration approaches, pattern-recognition myoelectric control, and mechatronic development of dexterous terminal devices; notes many remain preclinical or not widely accessible and that BP devices also need incremental R&D [5]"
"Extrapolation of Emerging Technologies and Their Long-Term Implications for Myoelectric versus Body-Powered Prostheses: An Engineering Perspective ","J Prosthet Orthot. ","2017 ","Narrative review (engineering perspective review article). ","Discusses ~50 years of incremental developments in body-powered and myoelectric upper extremity prostheses and the transition toward technologies providing sensory feedback and embodiment ","Body-powered and myoelectric prostheses; discusses sensory-feedback-enabled devices and emerging hybrid solutions ","Prosthetic rehabilitation focusing on functional use, embodiment, and incorporation into body image; implications for treatment selection between myoelectric and body-powered devices ","Emerging sensory feedback technologies are poised to promote embodiment and improve user perception of prostheses as true limb replacements; after decades of incremental change, these innovations could be transformative ","Innovations providing sensory feedback, technologies enhancing embodiment, and engineering advances in control and interface likely to change long-term prosthetic outcomes "
