Prosthetic Myoelectric Control Strategies: A Clinical Perspective [9],Not mentioned in source.,2014 [9],Narrative review (review paper discussing development and clinical perspective rather than systematic methods) [9],Discusses historical evolution from mid-1940s electronic myoelectric hands pioneered post-World War Two (Reinhold Reiter) and subsequent development of on/off and proportional control strategies through decades up to pattern recognition introduction around 1970 and Hudgins et al. 1993 advances [9],"Covers myoelectric control strategies including on/off (threshold), proportional (direct) control, pattern recognition, regression algorithms, targeted muscle reinnervation and implanted/high-density EMG methods — i.e., clinically-used myoelectric (surface), implanted/HD EMG, and surgical enhancement (TMR). Passive and body-powered prostheses are not discussed in detail. [9]","Discusses clinical applications: surface myoelectric control for hand open/close and wrist movement, TMR for creating additional control sites enabling multifunctional control, use of virtual rehabilitation environments (VREs) for training and assessment, and implanted peripheral nerve or cortical recordings as experimental/early clinical approaches [9]","Main conclusions: clinically available control remains dominated by simple proportional/on-off strategies despite research advances; regression algorithms show promise for simultaneous and proportional control; sensory feedback (tactile) and VREs are important for improving control and acceptance; many advanced approaches remain laboratory-based with barriers to clinical translation (reliability, electrode shift, real-world variability) [9]","Highlights technological advances: pattern recognition, regression algorithms (NMF, neural networks, linear regression), high-density and implanted EMG, targeted muscle reinnervation, tactile/haptic feedback and virtual rehabilitation environments; notes limitations for clinical adoption due to robustness and real-world variability [9]"
The importance of rehabilitation concerning upper extremity amputees: A Systematic review [10],Pakistan Journal of Medical Sciences (PJMS) [10],2016 [10],Systematic review [10],"Discusses recent technological advances and developments including new materials, sensorial and motor control restoration techniques such as targeted reinnervation and hand transplantation [10]","Mentions myoelectric/control-enabled prostheses (MyoBoy, DEKA) and implies technologically advanced arms; does not explicitly list passive or body-powered categories [10]","Prosthetic rehabilitation for functional restoration and independence, treatment of phantom limb pain, training for myoelectric prosthesis control, and preparation/rehabilitation for osseointegration-based prostheses [10]","Prosthetic rehabilitation appears promising for upper limb amputees, can alleviate phantom limb pain (supported by RCT), but existing evidence is limited and largely subjective with generally low methodological quality [10]","Highlights MyoBoy and DEKA myoelectric systems, virtual reality for training, targeted muscle reinnervation, hand transplantation, and osseointegration as notable technological advancements [10]"
