An overview and comparison of upper limb prosthetics [12],Not mentioned in source.,2013 [12],Not mentioned in source.,The paper briefly discusses progress over the preceding decade and provides comparisons between current and past upper limb prostheses [12].,"Covers commercial and research prostheses, with specific focus on myoelectric devices and haptic-feedback-enabled systems; implies discussion of current and past device types (e.g., passive, body-powered, myoelectric, hybrid) though not explicitly listed in the summary [12].",Discusses commercial achievements and shortcomings relevant to clinical application and deployment of upper limb prostheses [12].,Identifies myoelectric control as the primary limiting factor for progress in upper limb prosthetics [12].,Highlights recent research progress and recommends development of haptic feedback and advanced control algorithms as key technological directions [12].
Myoelectric Control for Upper Limb Prostheses [13],Electronics [13],2019 [13],Narrative review (review of recent literature) [13],Discusses historical use of EMG since the 1940s and contextual statistics on amputation prevalence; traces evolution from basic two-channel commercial systems to advanced machine-learning control approaches in recent decades [13],"Covers myoelectric prostheses (EMG-driven), body-powered/simple commercial two-electrode devices (sequential classification switching), hybrid approaches combining classification and regression, and surgical augmentations like Targeted Muscle Re-innervation (TMR) to provide signal sources [13]","Discusses clinical application in activities of daily living testing (grasping, clothespin relocation), use in transhumeral and shoulder-disarticulation amputees aided by TMR, and focus on improving long-term daily-life robustness and usability [13]","Main conclusions: research shows high lab performance but limited clinical translation due to robustness/non-stationarity issues; co-adaptation, transfer learning, and improved feedback are promising avenues; regression and hybrid control may offer more natural control but need robustness improvement for real-world use [13]","Highlights: machine-learning advances (regression, CNNs, rCNNs), co-adaptive algorithms, TMR, embedded implementations of novel models, and enabling technologies like 3D printing, low-power processors, IoT/5G and cloud computing for data fusion and transfer learning [13]"
"Prosthetic Outcome Measures for Use With Upper Limb Amputees: A Systematic Review of the Peer-Reviewed Literature, 1970 to 2009 [14]",Not mentioned in source.,2009 [14],Systematic review [14],"Covers literature and outcome-measure development from 1970 to March 2009, summarizing evolution of outcome measurement approaches and noting historical shifts toward Rasch analysis and standardized psychometric evaluation [14]","Discusses myoelectric and body-powered prostheses explicitly (e.g., measures specific to myoelectric control) and more generally covers prosthetic devices/terminal devices across categories used in studies (implicit coverage of passive and hybrid devices via device-focused evaluations) [14]","Clinical applications discussed include assessment of myoelectric prosthesis control, evaluation of terminal device effectiveness and hand/prehension function, assessment of prosthesis usefulness in daily bimanual activities, and measurement of participation and quality of life in adult and pediatric amputees [14]","Identifies ACMC, OPUS UEFS module, DASH, and TAPES as measures with greatest psychometric promise; notes sparse use of standardized outcome measures in adults; pediatric measures more developed and recommends further reliability and responsiveness testing for promising measures [14]",Notes rapid technological advancements (including DARPA initiatives and industry developments) that may change evaluation focus and enable more complex hand movements; suggests ongoing development of more functional and innovative prosthetic hands will affect outcome measurement needs [14]
"Real-Time EMG Based Pattern Recognition Control for Hand Prostheses: A Review on Existing Methods, Challenges and Future Implementation [15]",Sensors (Basel) [15],2019 [15],Narrative review (review article based on literature survey) [15],"Covers evolution of EMG-based pattern recognition from late 1960s development through 1980s refinement, 1990s neural network advances, and increasing research since 2000s culminating in intensive work after 2010 [15]","Focuses on myoelectric prostheses: distinguishes pattern-recognition-based myoelectric control and non-pattern-recognition (proportional/on-off) myoelectric control; discusses multi-DOF myoelectric hands, embedded systems and virtual/real implementations. Passive and body-powered prostheses not discussed in detail in this source [15]","Discusses clinical/real-world applications of EMG-PR myoelectric prostheses for daily living tasks, use with commercial multi-DOF arms (e.g., DEKA), implementation in TMR patients and tests on amputees including virtual training and embedded system control [15]","Main conclusions: EMG-PR shows strong offline accuracy but reduced performance in real-world amputee use; challenges include electrode shift, limb position, muscle fatigue, embedded system constraints, and lack of proportional/simultaneous multi-DOF control; adaptive/intelligent, low-cost, and multimodal sensing (e.g., IMU, FMG, ultrasound, TMR) and deep learning suggested as future directions [15]","Highlights: shift from gel to dry electrodes and high-density sensors; advanced feature extraction (TD, AR, wavelets, TD-PSD, TSD), classifiers (LDA, SVM, ANN, deep learning potential), embedded real-time processors, integration of IMU/FM G/ultrasound, TMR and implantable sensors, and proposals for shielding, adaptive algorithms and deep learning to improve robustness [15]"
